            ("p", "`site.style(name, rules)` registers a new, reusable class from a plain `{css-property: value}` dict -- never a raw CSS string, the same boundary ARKlight holds everywhere else. Calling it again with a name already used overwrites that class's rules."),
            ("code", "site = Site()\nsite.style(\"pull-quote\", {\"font-style\": \"italic\", \"border-left\": \"4px solid purple\"})\n\n@site.page(\"/\")\ndef home():\n    return Page(Text(\"A quote worth repeating.\", class_name=\"pull-quote\"))"),
            ("h2", "Layout without @media"),
            ("p", "`Page` never gets a `<head>` hook for a stylesheet of your own, and nothing in the default CSS is keyed to a viewport width, device class or browser engine. Layouts still adapt, but from the content's own available width, using plain flexbox/grid sizing keywords -- the technique goes by \"intrinsic web design.\" Opt in with `class_name`, the same mechanism as `.card` above."),
